hi guys it's me quinn and as always if  you appreciate my content consider  hitting the like button it's the only  way the youtube algorithm really notices  me i apologize for the format of this  video i am on the move and i don't have  everything i need with me i'm gonna be  seeing dune in a couple days  but i wanted to talk about something  before i saw the movie because it's  something that i think a lot of people  still  have questions about and don't really  get and it's one of the main questions  that i get  um about dune from people and it's the  question of why doesn't dune  have any type of computers or a.i or  robots and the answer to that is  multifaceted but ultimately i think  pretty simple so you had asimov before  frank herbert who came in and said i'm  going to take science fiction but i'm  going to expand it  an entire galaxy i'm going to stretch it  out through a cosmos and he asked  himself how would one manage an empire  that was comprised of thousands of  planets stretched out across the vacuum  of space and the answer was not very  well but to some degree the answer was  also  robots and artificial intelligence now  when frank herbert came along a few  decades later he did several things to  distinguish himself from isaac asimov  but one of the things he did was he said  i'm not going to put any type of ai and  robots in my series now ai and robots do  exist in the dune universe but in the  original frank herbert books they are  part of the  long past history there were once  robots thinking machines as doom refers  to them as but they went through this  thing called the butlerian jihad which  was basically when  mankind rebelled against the machine  overlords who themselves were being  controlled by other men  so it's not necessarily the whole  terminator idea where it's machines gain  sentience and try to rebel against  humanity it's more to do with  the men in power of those machines using  those machines to control the rest of  humanity which is something that i think  is shockingly relevant for our society  right now and the other aspect of it  that i think is super relevant was that  humankind was using machines as a crutch  because we had machines it was kind of  just stifling our own development so  after the machines went away human kinds  had to find other ways to hone their  skills and different schools popped up  like the mentat school who are human  computers and the benny jesuit  sisterhood or these women that train  themselves and hone their abilities to  the point where they're almost  superhuman there's the spacing guild  whose mutated navigators took the place  of the computers that were once used to  navigate throughout the vacuum of space  so essentially frank herbert is focusing  on the potential evolution of humanity  rather than muddying things up with  machines now of course brian herbert  frank herbert's son and kevin j anderson  who continue with the books in the  expanded dune series have a totally  different philosophy and they actually  went back and filled in some of that  history and the machine crusade trilogy  and they also continue the  machine thing in the later books which  were meant to finish off the original  franker  six books which were never completed  but that still doesn't change the fact  that the philosophy of those original  six books is that we're going to focus  on humans and the human struggle  rather than having it be something  that's not human that's doing it to us  because really humanity is usually its  own  biggest enemy  right so we see that also play out in  the later two frank herbert books with  the returning of the people from the  scattering uh essentially people that  have left the empire thousands of years  ago come back to the old empire to  basically cannibalize the old empire and  it's always humanity feeding upon itself  so i think that there's an argument to  be made that sometimes when you  introduce  this non-human element and say this is  the cause of everything  it kind of cheapens the whole thing  because you're relieving humanity itself  of responsibility so to break things  down again as simply as possible there's  no  robots or ai in the original six dune  books because  one  it's a human story  two  humanity used computers and robots and  stuff like that as a crutch  and three frank herbert wanted to  distinguish himself from writers that  came before him like asimov and others  by not using computers or robots  and it's also true that because frank  herbert decided to go this route there's  so many interesting things narratively  speaking that arise like you have the  telexu who have this emphasis on  biological technology and all sorts of  absurd and grotesque things arise out of  that  so i hope that clears things up because  like i said that's one of the number one  questions that i get on this channel and  also i just wanted to say thanks  everyone for liking and subscribing it's  been really exciting to see all the new  people interested in dune since the  movie is about to come out in the united  states and china it's been really  awesome to see some of my videos being  shared on websites like av club and  boing boing and i'm just super anxious  and excited to see the movie just two  days away from me and then i can feast  my eyes upon dune thanks so much for  watching guys peace out  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

